  :adr/body
  "## Context

The owner asked whether this workspace already holds a repository that
preserves sanction facts — who did what, historically and objectively, kept
permanently, in the manner of the biblical Books of Kings. It does, in three
layers with different jobs:

* `cloud-itonami/kosatsu` (高札) — attributed crime and sanctions designations,
  delistings and competing claims. Preserves authority attribution and event
  history, exposes disagreement, issues no verdict and no score.
* `kotoba-lang/innen` (因縁) + `loop-innen` — a sourced dependency record over
  human history; bitemporal, BCE-capable, unsourced edges inadmissible.
* `cloud-itonami/danjo` (弾正) — factual, non-adjudicating observation of
  public government data.

`cloud-itonami/sanctions` is the closest NAME and the emptiest tree: zero data
files, two disjoint implementations that disagree on schema, and a live
contradiction about whether screening belongs in it that the repository does not
resolve. It is not the place to go for this subject.

The Kings comparison is exact enough to be useful. That book does three things:
records each reign in order, cites its sources (`the annals of the kings of
Israel`), and JUDGES. This workspace holds the first two in kosatsu and innen
and deliberately withholds the third — kosatsu is chartered `never issues a
verdict or score`, danjo is non-adjudicating. The only repository that judges is
`etzhayyim/openicc`, chartered the day before, and it cannot publish a named
finding alone. The division is the same one Kings has between the annals and the
Deuteronomistic historian who assessed them.

What was missing was durability. kosatsu's log is a content-addressed commit
DAG, so tampering is detectable BY ANYONE HOLDING THE LOG. It cannot prove to a
third party that a head existed at a time: whoever controls the file can rewrite
the chain from genesis and hand out a consistent lie. Gate G4 (`a delisting is a
NEW datom; nothing is overwritten`) was therefore a promise rather than a
checkable property.

## Decision

### D1 — delegate anchoring; do not grow a second anchor format

`kotoba-lang/kotobase-anchor-fevm` already owns chain-neutral checkpoint
receipts, and its DEFAULT provider is a signed append-only transparency log
requiring no chain, no gas and no RPC; public-chain notarisation is optional.
`kosatsu.methods.anchor` delegates to it.

The reason is the same one the repository already applies to itself: 高札
authors no designation of its own, and a consensus or anchor plane must not hold
two answers to the question it exists to answer. This was the first thing
checked, and finding the existing implementation changed the design — the
alternative was writing an anchor record format inside kosatsu.

### D2 — an anchored head makes G4 falsifiable

If the record ever moves under a previously anchored head, the commitment no
longer opens. That converts `nothing is overwritten` from a statement about our
intentions into a statement a stranger can test.

### D3 — G10-anchor: representative records may not reach a public chain

Article G10 requires `:representative` versus `:authoritative` on every datom.
From outside, a commitment over the synthetic seed and one over real
designations are THE SAME 32 BYTES. A public-chain artefact is permanent and
carries no room to say `this was synthetic`, so notarising the seed there would
leave a durable, externally citable record implying 高札 had captured real
designations.

Therefore `:evm` is refused unless the log is wholly `:authoritative`, and the
sourcing mode is carried INSIDE the commitment: `database-id` is
`kosatsu/representative`, `kosatsu/authoritative`, `kosatsu/mixed` or
`kosatsu/unsourced`. A receipt separated from its context still says which kind
of record it covers.

### D4 — the anchor carries a commitment, never the record

`{database-id, epoch, logical-checkpoint-root}`; the root is an opaque CID. No
subject label, no authority id, no datom crosses it. G5 (subject-dignity /
no-doxxing) would be violated by an anchor that leaked who is designated, and
the anchor is the one artefact here designed to be permanent and public. This is
CHECKED (`discloses-nothing?`) rather than asserted, because the payload shape is
owned upstream and could grow without anything here noticing.

### D5 — three more refusals, each with a reason

* an empty log is `{:ok? false :checked 0 :reason :empty-log}` — checked-zero
  must never read as verified-intact
* `checkpoint` throws on an unverified chain rather than returning nil, because
  a caller that ignores a nil anchors nothing and reports success
* `provider` is required with no default — a defaulted destination is a
  destination nobody chose, and one of the two is irreversible

### D6 — the test gate was measuring 4% of itself, and that is now fixed

`clojure -M:test` ran `kosatsu.murakumo-test` ALONE — 9 tests, 31 of 729
assertions — and printed `0 failures`. The cognitect runner defaults to
`#\".*-test$\"` and twelve of thirteen namespaces are named
`kosatsu.methods.test-*`. The alias now passes `-d test -r \".*\"`.

Fixing it surfaced two breaks that only `bb test` had hidden, both because
babashka is more permissive than the JVM: `cheshire` missing from the test
classpath (bb bundles it) and three tests calling a `defn-` var directly (SCI
does not enforce privacy). cheshire is KEPT rather than replaced: `ingest.cljc`
ships a self-contained JSON reader on purpose, and cheshire is the independent
writer its fixtures come from, so the reader is checked against someone else's
JSON instead of against itself.

## Consequences

- The committed seed is `:representative`, so every plan kosatsu produces today
  is `kosatsu/representative` and the public chain is closed to it by
  G10-anchor. That is the design working, not a limitation to route around.
- Live full-universe ingest (OFAC SDN / EU consolidated / UN SC / UK OFSI /
  JP-MOF / Interpol) remains G8-gated: Council Lv6+ plus operator plus member
  signature. That is a governance gate, not a technical one, and the standing
  authorisation for agent-judged external effects does not cover it.
- No adapter is deployed. Nothing has been anchored. `plan` returns a plan.
- `etzhayyim/openicc` still carries its OWN `anchor-record` shape, written
  before `kotobase-anchor-fevm` was found. That is exactly the second
  implementation D1 refuses, now sitting in a sibling repository, and it is the
  first item of unfinished work from this session.

## Evidence

Measured 2026-08-22 at `cloud-itonami/kosatsu` 7d8ebed:

- `clojure -M:test` — 173 tests / 729 assertions, 0 failures (was 9 / 31)
- breaking the G10-anchor guard reds exactly
  `test-representative-records-are-refused-a-public-chain`; breaking the
  empty-log floor reds `test-empty-log-is-not-intact`. Both restored to green.
- `clojure -M:lint` — 13 errors across 8 files, ALL pre-existing and in files
  this change does not touch (`src/kosatsu/mesh.clj` 3, `ingest.cljc`,
  `weave.cljc`, five test namespaces). The two new files contribute none. The
  lint gate was already red before this change and remains so; recorded in the
  README rather than silently carried."}]
